We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x+2$ to get $x^2+7x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x+5$:

$\blueD{(x+2)(x+5)=x^2+7x+10}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2+7x+12}{x+2}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2+7x+10})+2}{x+2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+7x+10}}{x+2}+\dfrac{2}{x+2}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x+2)(x+5)=x^2+7x+10}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2+7x+10}}{x+2}+\dfrac{2}{x+2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x+2)}}\blueD{(x+5)}}{\cancel{x+2}}+\dfrac{2}{x+2}
\\\\
&=x+5+\dfrac{2}{x+2}
\end{align}$
